WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.519
You open a map on your phone, and a blue
dot settles on the street where you are

2
00:00:03.599 --> 00:00:04.599
standing.

3
00:00:04.772 --> 00:00:08.871
Nobody typed your address, so the phone
worked the dot out from signals arriving

4
00:00:08.951 --> 00:00:09.951
on its own.

5
00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:15.632
You already use this idea when a flash
of lightning arrives before its thunder.

6
00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:20.926
Counting the seconds between them
tells you how far away the strike was,

7
00:00:21.344 --> 00:00:23.863
because sound travels at a steady speed.

8
00:00:24.409 --> 00:00:27.660
That delay gives one distance,
not a location.

9
00:00:28.622 --> 00:00:33.558
Each GPS satellite broadcasts a radio
signal stamped with the time it was sent,

10
00:00:33.638 --> 00:00:34.556
plus information

11
00:00:34.636 --> 00:00:36.494
about its orbit. Your phone

12
00:00:37.004 --> 00:00:41.905
notes when that signal arrives, so travel
time multiplied by the speed of light

13
00:00:41.985 --> 00:00:42.985
gives a range estimate.

14
00:00:44.957 --> 00:00:50.089
That estimate is not yet exact, because
the phone's own clock drifts a little.

15
00:00:50.541 --> 00:00:55.870
Until that clock error is accounted for,
these distances are called pseudoranges.

16
00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.376
This picture is a
two-dimensional schematic.

17
00:00:59.921 --> 00:01:03.219
One range places you on a
circle around its satellite.

18
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.469
A second range, holding at the same time,

19
00:01:06.853 --> 00:01:09.720
leaves two possibilities
where the circles cross.

20
00:01:10.487 --> 00:01:14.109
The real three-dimensional
problem uses spheres instead,

21
00:01:14.446 --> 00:01:16.779
and must also solve the clock error.

22
00:01:18.887 --> 00:01:20.338
Now count the unknowns:

23
00:01:20.791 --> 00:01:24.947
your three coordinates in space,
plus the error in your phone's clock,

24
00:01:25.226 --> 00:01:27.002
which we call the clock offset.

25
00:01:27.699 --> 00:01:31.926
Four independent measurements from four
satellites in suitable positions are

26
00:01:32.006 --> 00:01:37.070
solved together for all four at once,
so your phone needs no atomic clock of its

27
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:37.987
own.

28
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:40.656
Real receivers often use more than four.

29
00:01:42.754 --> 00:01:46.620
Take the speed of light as three
hundred million metres per second.

30
00:01:47.166 --> 00:01:50.591
If a signal's time is off by
one millionth of a second,

31
00:01:51.125 --> 00:01:56.129
multiply the two numbers, and the range
error is about three hundred metres.

32
00:01:56.941 --> 00:02:00.912
That is a range error,
not a guaranteed position error.

33
00:02:02.252 --> 00:02:07.037
It is tempting to think three satellites
pin you down and a fourth just measures

34
00:02:07.117 --> 00:02:08.117
altitude.

35
00:02:08.312 --> 00:02:12.782
That is not how it works, because your
phone's clock error is unknown too,

36
00:02:13.119 --> 00:02:17.252
so the measurements are solved together,
not one satellite per job.

37
00:02:18.592 --> 00:02:20.798
So the blue dot comes from timing.

38
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:26.603
A signal's travel time gives a range,
several ranges narrow the possibilities,

39
00:02:26.986 --> 00:02:31.885
and four independent measurements from
well-placed satellites are solved together

40
00:02:32.257 --> 00:02:35.113
for three coordinates
and the clock offset.

41
00:02:35.844 --> 00:02:41.278
Real receivers often use more, and
accuracy also depends on the atmosphere,

42
00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:44.273
reflected signals and satellite geometry.
